1 INTRODUCTION
Researchers have been applying data mining and machine learning techniques to security challenges for the past 20 years or so. A search of the bibliographic database DBLP1 with the query intrusion detection neural network led to 216 matches on August 21, 2019. Note that this result is for just one machine learning technique and just one security challenge. The mind boggles when one considers the plethora of data mining and machine learning techniques and the variety of security challenges, which have no good separations between legitimate instances and attack instances. These include phishing, malware, stepping-stone detection, intrusion detection, and denial of service attacks, just to name a few.
However, some researchers had observed that the overwhelming majority of this research is simply not being deployed [17]. So, the question arises: “Why is there such a gap between theory and practice in security?” The reasons seem to be mainly two:
• Researchers, in their work, have missed at least some of the unique needs of the security domain. For a discussion on these aspects, see [3, 20].
